{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /workspace/paper/.claude/skills/retrieve-references\n\n# What the skill is about\nFetch references from Semantic Scholar Graph API, OpenAlex, or Google Scholar (via `scholarly`) and keep only real BibTeX entries.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final report/paper generation.\n- Related-work section needs grounded citations.\n\n# What to do\n1. Build focused search queries from task goal, method names, and top experiment ideas.\n2. Preferred path (Semantic Scholar Graph API):\n```bash\npython scripts/s2_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n3. Fallback path (OpenAlex):\n```bash\npython scripts/openalex_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 10 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Optional:\n   - `--mailto you@example.com` to enter the OpenAlex polite pool\n   - Env fallback: `OPENALEX_EMAIL`\n4. Alternate path (Google Scholar API wrapper with `scholarly`):\n```bash\npython -m pip install scholarly\npython scripts/scholarly_fetch.py \\\n  --query \"query one\" \\\n  --query \"query two\" \\\n  --min-refs 8 \\\n  --max-refs 15 \\\n  --search-limit 25 \\\n  --sort-by relevance \\\n  --proxy-mode auto \\\n  --delay-seconds 0.5 \\\n  --out-bib references.bib \\\n  --out-json references_provenance.json\n```\n   Proxy options:\n   - `--proxy-mode auto|none|free|single`\n   - `--proxy-http <url>` / `--proxy-https <url>` for `single`\n   - Env fallbacks: `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_MODE`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTP`, `SCHOLARLY_PROXY_HTTPS`\n   - Global `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` are intentionally ignored to avoid accidental ambient proxying\n5. Require `S2_API_KEY` when available for `s2_fetch.py` (script reads env automatically).\n6. Keep only references with valid BibTeX and citation key.\n7. Dedupe by citation key and paper id.\n8. Fail hard if references found `< min-refs`.\n9. Use `s2_fetch.py` first for stability, `openalex_fetch.py` as the next fallback, and `scholarly_fetch.py` last if the other sources fail.\n\n# Outputs\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n\n# Notes\n- Use `templates/provenance.schema.json` for expected shape.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"648bc85b-8fdb-45ce-a1f2-45a49bd72b70","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:29:43.342Z","isSynthetic":true}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwJNMmmdZA8icYGjEaKR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kgq1hVgohuSWaKqVE9XfhU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"ls .claude/skills/retrieve-references/ .claude/skills/retrieve-references/scripts 2>/dev/null; sed -n 1,80p paper.tex | head -5; grep -n \"Parametrization and optimizer\\|Relation to this work\\|capacity-based\\|cluster bootstrap\\|censored data\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3470,"cache_read_input_tokens":50649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3470},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"2bb7d7a8-47c4-4235-807d-a7bfe01584db","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:29:45.245Z","request_id":"req_011CgwJNLpiiK9QreQuQyDvz"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kgq1hVgohuSWaKqVE9XfhU","type":"tool_result","content":".claude/skills/retrieve-references/:\nSKILL.md\nscripts\ntemplates\n\n.claude/skills/retrieve-references/scripts:\nopenalex_fetch.py\ns2_fetch.py\nscholarly_fetch.py\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n45:In small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n47:This paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n56:\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n64:\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization of training data has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}, and a method for estimating how much information a model stores about data points has been proposed and measured on language models \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n66:\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n121:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\n135:95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\n256:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n282:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use enough seeds that the cluster bootstrap has a rich resampling support, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"34e103e8-5a51-4bf0-bc21-268235ec741f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:29:45.330Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":".claude/skills/retrieve-references/:\nSKILL.md\nscripts\ntemplates\n\n.claude/skills/retrieve-references/scripts:\nopenalex_fetch.py\ns2_fetch.py\nscholarly_fetch.py\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTe