{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R51JGkUhpYCnzgN59MPBYB","type":"tool_result","content":"\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the trai\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\n\n\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization of training data has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}, and a method for estimating how much information a model stores about data points has been proposed and measured on language models \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n\n\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n\n\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.} Each seed fixes both the train/held-out split and the weight initialization, and the design has exactly one run per seed and width. Resampling seeds therefore propagates split and initialization variability jointly, and a seed is the unit of replication; run-to-run nondeterminism within a seed is not separated from it, and cannot be, since no seed is repeated at a width in the sealed design. Because the design is balanced, the pooled OLS slope equals the slope fitted to the width-wise means of $\\ln\\Delta$, and the bootstrap interval is the percentile range of that quantity over seed resamples.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n\\label{sec:model}\nThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. Writing $z_i=E[x_i]+P_i$ for the embedded tokens $x_1,x_2,x_3{=}$``$=$'', the evaluated model computes\n\\begin{equation}\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), i.e.\\ runs that end below $0.99$ held-out accuracy are censored however close they come to it. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n\n\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"ec05c456-310f-44e3-8fc8-e7463be423a9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:32:02.799Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the trai\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{N