{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwJYdizdvL6FLrUghSz8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YcU2wFLMdhYM1UpBsZ9eMe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:40]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\n\n# Related work: prior empirical grokking studies\nrep(\"These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\",\n\"These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. Empirical studies have varied some of those knobs on modular arithmetic or related algorithmic tasks: \\\\citet{Doshi2023ToGO} study modular arithmetic with corrupted labels and examine how generalization and memorization coexist under regularization; \\\\citet{Thilak2022TheSM} associate grokking under adaptive optimizers with cyclic behavior of the last-layer norm; and \\\\citet{Prieto2025GrokkingAT} attribute the absence of grokking without regularization to numerical instability in the softmax. We do not re-derive these findings, and none of them is, to our reading, a measurement of delay against width under a fixed protocol, which is the quantity examined here; the present study does not test the accounts against each other.\")\n\n# Parametrization paragraph\nrep(\"motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role\",\n\"motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The width-aware parametrization literature makes this precise: \\\\citet{Yang2021TensorPI} characterize a maximal-update parametrization in which hidden-layer features keep learning as width grows, and \\\\citet{Yang2022TensorPV} show that hyperparameters such as the learning rate then transfer across widths. Our multipliers resemble this construction in spirit, but we do not claim that they implement it, and we did not check hyperparameter transfer; the point for this study is only that a width-dependent multiplier can change how fast wider models move, and so can mask or create a width slope. The role\")\n\n# Method: bootstrap\nrep(\"(a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed).\",\n\"(a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\\\citealp{Cameron2015APG}).\")\nrep(\"\\\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.}\",\n\"\\\\paragraph{Caveat on few clusters.} The cluster-robust inference literature documents that inference with a small number of clusters can be unreliable, and that refinements to the basic cluster bootstrap, such as the wild cluster bootstrap-$t$, can improve coverage \\\\citep{Cameron2008BootstrapBasedIF,Cameron2015APG}. We use the plain percentile interval with four clusters as pre-declared, make no claim of nominal coverage, and treat the interval as a coarse summary of seed-to-seed variability rather than an exact confidence statement.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.}\")\n\n# what would resolve\nrep(\"(ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise\",\n\"(ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\\\ \\\\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"citealp\\|usepackage.*natbib\" paper.tex | head -3; grep -n \"Honest\\|capacity-based reading predicts\" paper.tex | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3368,"cache_read_input_tokens":62463,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3368},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"55194dc8-9e8e-4543-bad0-a29ea0d0829a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:32:12.899Z","request_id":"req_011CgwJYd5oCYdqYAPiWrEUL"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YcU2wFLMdhYM1UpBsZ9eMe","type":"tool_result","content":"83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n258:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap (survival-analysis regression, e.g.\\ \\citealp{Cox1972RegressionMA}, handles right-censored outcomes directly, although we did not apply it), or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n45:In small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0e38efc-073d-49e4-ba45-78862c4702dc","uuid":"835c5242-efbf-48b7-8730-cada97602d5f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:32:13.001Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed; \\citealp{Cameron2015APG}). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies en